e should do something  about it who is we  united farm workers union and the  growers  talked to each other for over a year to  try and  see if we could come up with a path  forward  republicans and i want to call out  specifically congressman  dan newhouse and mike simpson as well as  many others  and myself and jimmy panetta and jimmy  costanza  and many others work together to come up  with a compromise that will allow  farm workers to gain legal status and  also to regularize the existing  h2a program so that farmworkers in the  future  can come over in a legal and orderly  manner  this bill is a compromise of into  bipartisan compromise  it's a compromise between farmers  and farm workers it's something i hope  that will pass a bipartisan support  if it does america will be stronger  than it is today i yield now to  a wonderful person who's worked so hard  on the  tps matter and that is the chairwoman of  velasquez good morning everyone thank  you  uh cheerleading and um  i want to thank chairman ruth  speaker for osce leader hoyer  and all the other amazing advocates for  humane  immigration reform and let me just  remind  everyone immigration  and immigrants make america  america and to all the activists  the grassroots organization who stood up  and stood firm to demand a more  just immigration system this bill are  made possible  by you as a co-author of the dream and  promise act along with representative  royga  allard and yvette clark i am proud that  this bill  will shield qualifying recipients of cps  tps ded and daca from deportation  and create a more comprehensive pathway  to citizenship these are our co-workers  friends families and hard-working  law-abiding members of our community  this deal is the morally right thing to  do  is the fiscally responsible move and  most of all  it is the american way forward thank you  and this is one thing  [Music]  thank you very much i want to thank  chairman  ruiz for bringing us together today  lucille roybal lillard and zoe lofgren  and all the others assembled here for  sponsoring the two bills we're  considering this afternoon  and of course our distinguished leaders  speaker pelosi and majority leader hoyer  for bringing these important bills to  the floor  after closing the book on four years of  disastrous cruel and inhumane  immigration policies today we begin a  new chapter  it is one based on compassion on reason  and the fundamental values we hold dear  as americans  all of the individuals who would gain  protection under the bills we're  considering today  dreamers tps and ded recipients and farm  workers  are vital members of our communities as  though said without the immigrant farm  workers  the undocumented immigrant farm workers  we couldn't grow our crops and feed our  people  they are our neighbors our friends our  classmates our service members and our  essential workers  they are driving forces in our economy  and in our culture  the two bills we will pass today  recognize that america is stronger  when we welcome people to our shores  later this afternoon the house has sent  a clear message  that we believe in a just rational and  humane immigration system  i hope that our senate colleagues will  hear us on the other side of the capitol  and will take similar actions i'm proud  to stand with my colleagues here today  and i look forward to sending both of  these important bills to the president's  desk  let me uh thank chairman ruiz speaker  pelosi  my colleagues congressman lucille roybal  allard  congresswoman nydia velasquez of course  chairwoman law friend and chairman jerry  nadler  and all of my colleagues in the chc i'm  conference committee betsy clark  i proudly represent new york's ninth  congressional district  central and south brooklyn as the  daughter of jamaican immigrants  i understand the need the need for the  dream  and promise act and a 21st century  immigration system  worthy of our great nation  let me be clear our current immigration  system  is broken and to coin a phrase  of a another very distinguished  jamaican-american the notorious b.i.g  if you don't know now you know  imagine being bought to the united  states of america  you're on a road trip with the family  you're a child  you're observing your surroundings  you come to a new place you settle  you grow up with other children you  dream he was fired you reach your goals  you're educated you're engaged  only to find out you're undocumented  only to find out that the work that you  have done to help build this country  has not been given the same value as if  you were born  in this nation today  we are saying let us continue to build  on the american dream  let us pass the dream and promise act we  have an  obligation to bring forth  the type of support undergirding that  needs to happen for those who have been  a lifeline  to so many families across this nation  not only  during this pandemic before it and will  continue to do so  as proud americans as we passed the  dream of promising  with that i think it was a minute i  yield back  thank you good morning everyone thank  you representative clark chairman ruiz  and speaker pelosi for this opportunity  to speak in support  of the american dream and promise act i  am proudly a member of both the  congressional black caucus  and the congressional asia pacific  islander caucus here in u.s congress  for the 1.5 million asian dreamers and  2.1 million african immigrants  the american dream and promise act isn't  just any piece of legislation  it is a pathway to citizenship that will  determine  their future in this country in this  place that they call home  in this place for millions of people who  have known no other home but the united  states  and i want to talk a bit about the  recipients who are going to come from  korea  and from the continent of africa koreans  are one of the first groups to sign up  for daca when it was announced by  president obama  and immigrants from africa from nigeria  ethiopia  ghana encountered to name a few are now  among the fastest  growing group of immigrants across the  united states creating a viable pathway  to citizenship  and acceptance is something that all  undocumented immigrants  grapple with i want to thank the  congressional hispanic caucus for  leading the way on this issue  and i want to encourage everyone to  support  these bills and i say this because we  talk about bipartisanship here in  congress  but these bills are wildly popular with  the american public  america wants these things to happen  we've been talking about this for too  long  and now is the time to act it's the  morally right thing to do  it's the courageous thing to do that's  good for the economy  and it epitomizes what it means to be  american i know  you to representative thank you  thank you madam speaker and uh  chairman louise uh sure woman uh  royal holler chair woman uh solo after  for your leadership and your efforts in  bringing us together  to uh this important day and it is an  important day it's a good day  for uh reform in our immigration system  in this country  as was noted we have a broken  immigration system and it's been that  way for decades  and it's time we get about the work of  fixing it  the legislation that we will put on  today will cover  almost half or maybe as much as of the  undocumented people that we have in our  country today  and give them legal status  let me start where i think i come from  food is a national security issue it's  not often looked at that way  we take it for granted but certainly in  the last 12 months with this pandemic  with closure of schools and restaurants  and our complicated complex food supply  chain turned upside down  i think more americans are beginning to  understand  how critical it is for the five percent  of the population the nation's  population  that puts food on america's dinner table  every night  i know because i'm a third generation  farmer  it's a partnership it always has been a  partnership between farm workers  farmers dairy men and women and  it's that partnership that has been part  of the great success of america  that allows us not only to feed all of  our people but to also produce  enough food for other parts of the world  where they are in need  let me tell you something the coalition  that came together was a result of the  hard work of a lot of folks here  the united farm workers who many of us  worked with for years  the grower organizations and much of  organized labor  understanding that farm workers  the compasinos who  make up such a wonderful part of our  country in the samuel king valley that i  represent  are some of the hardest working  individuals i have ever met  yes i know these cappucinos from the  time i was a young man  on my family's farm until an adult i  work side by side with them  i know what hard work is all about  and they are trying to do their best to  raise their families  and to have a better opportunity and a  brighter future for themselves and their  and for our country yet it is simply  wrong for them to live  and work under the shadow of uncertainty  and fear  of being deported that's what it is  it's that fear that they live with from  day to day  maria who i spoke with a few months ago  lived in parliament she picks  uh and works in an orange packing shed  as risen in the ranks there but she left  a few months ago from this good paying  job she purchased a home she had her  family because some of her family was  documented so was not  and therefore the word was out that the  immigration service was looking at  deporting and making a raid at this  packing champion  that's real that's fear so let me close  by saying that when we talk about some  of the hardest working men and women  and their young children who put food on  america's dinner table every night  today we are going to give them hope  the hope that they have that i see in  their eyes when i go to my district  every week  a hope for a normal life free from the  dread of family separation  family separation and deportation that  hangs over their head  every day when they leave to go to work  hope for a chance to change their  reality and reshape their story and live  the american green for ladies and  gentlemen  our nation is a land of immigrants past  and immigrants present i know  i'm a second generation immigrant my  grandparents came to this country  illiterate non-english speaking  as farm workers they melt cats  i have some skill sets that i still have  but the fact is is today we can make a  difference and i want to thank so much  so lucille and doctor  let me introduce one of my partners who  said  salute carvajal has historian he and i  represent some of the largest  agricultural regions  in california therefore in the country  jimmy panetta and so many others have  worked so hard in this effort  along with the new house congressman  salud  from the great central coast thank you  representative costa  let me start by thanking a few people  uh that really have brought us here  first i want to thank  dr reese for organizing today's event  but the two authors of these two  important bills  work extremely hard to get us here and i  want to recognize representative  laughter  representative allen for getting this  here  and let's face it nothing happens here  without  leadership not only from the individuals  i mentioned but  from our extraordinary speaker nancy  pelosi i want to thank them for getting  it  thank you very much i'm a  son of a farmworker many in summer  anxiety  toiled in the fields with my father i  know firsthand  the hard work of farm workers i also  happen to represent  a district where the number one industry  is agriculture  so making sure that our farm workers are  taken care of and that our  farmers uh have the tools and and  the assistance they need to prosper is  of the utmost importance to me  this is personal to me many of these  immigrants  via dreamers or farm workers come to  this country to live the american dream  come to ensure that their kids have a  better way of life  some of them even become members of  congress  so today is a good day for america  these bills are bipartisan we have  bipartisan members of congress that have  come together  and a diverse group of stakeholders  especially the farm workforce  modernization act  this is a bill that is historic when you  consider all of the groups that came  together to make it happen  today is an important day to not just  take all that our country and our  economy has been taking from immigrants  their essential work  to keep america working but recognizing  them  and giving them back that benefit  to live that american dream in this  country that they came here for  thank you very much  i want to close by giving my sincere  appreciation  adoration and respect to chair zoe  lofgren  and to chair lucille roybal allard  and to speaker pelosi and all the  members who spoke today members  representing uh  both capex and cbc you see that  the diversity not only that are up here  but all those that are affected by  this broken immigration system and  together we can get it fixed  thank you very much and that concludes  our press conference  [Music]  you
 

